<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#trans-saharan-trade">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trans-Saharan trade</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as trans-Saharan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The trans-Saharan trade was the network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> Desert linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a>. Gold, slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and other goods moved north, while salt, horses, textiles and manufactured goods moved south, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and literacy. It underpinned the wealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> empires, and earlier the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a>, as well as the Almoravid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> states to the north.</p></div>
